meta {
  name: Log Mailer Lead
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/mailers/log-lead
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "controlNumber": "{{mailerLongForm}}"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  // Captured for the replay request, which must return this same id.
  if (res.getStatus() === 200) {
    bru.setVar("mailerLeadId", res.getBody().leadId);
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    // 200, not 201: this resolves-or-creates. On a replay nothing was created.
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("returns the lead id and whether it already existed", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.leadId).to.be.a("string");
    expect(body.alreadyExisted).to.be.a("boolean");
    expect(Object.keys(body).sort()).to.eql(["alreadyExisted", "leadId"]);
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /mailers/log-lead — save a mailer's recipient as a lead (PAC-61)

  Modules `mailers` **and** `leads` · permissions **`mailers:read`** and
  **`leads:write`** (both required).

  One click in the Mailers drawer. Replaces legacy's three-step Mailers search
  page → mailer detail page → *Log Lead*.

  **Body**
  | field | type | rules |
  |---|---|---|
  | `controlNumber` | string | 1–80 chars after trim; either printed form, any case, any punctuation |

  That is the whole body. Everything written to the lead — the recipient, the
  address, the lead source, the producer — is read from the stored mailer or
  from the authenticated user, so there is nothing here for a caller to
  influence.

  ## What it writes

  Runs the **same** `LeadIntakeService` pipeline as `POST /leads` and the public
  share-link route, so contact matching, household derivation, dedupe, linking
  and assignment are written once: resolve contact → derive household → resolve
  or create lead → link → assign producer. A Lead, a Household and a Contact
  come out of a first call.

  - **Lead source is always `WCO7l` (Mailer)**, set server-side and never read
    from the request. Legacy branched to JYA on
    `Campaign_Number.startsWith('JYA')`; no campaign number in this data can
    match — every value is `Week_Number-NN` — so that branch is not ported.
  - **The producer is the caller.** Legacy resolved this through ~170 lines of
    Clerk lookup with an email fallback and a self-healing `PATCH`, all of which
    existed because it had no first-class user identity.
  - **`quoteControlNumber` gets the LONG form**, taken from the mailer document
    rather than from what was typed. Two reasons: the Leads list searches that
    field with a contains-regex and the short form is the last 12 characters
    *inside* the long one, so storing the long form is findable by either — and
    the dedupe on that field is an exact-equality **merge**, where a collision
    on 48 bits of truncated UUID would quietly join two different prospects'
    leads.
  - **The address stores the 5-digit zip**, not the ZIP+4. Address dedupe keys
    on `street|zip`, so a `74133-5807` would stop this lead ever matching one a
    producer typed by hand at the same house.
  - **No `policiesOfInterest`.** `campaign.policyType` says what *kind* of
    policy was mailed, but the row carries no item count and the field requires
    one. Writing `itemCount: 1` would put a guess where a producer later reads a
    captured answer.

  ### Why it does not reuse `create-lead.dto.ts`

  That DTO's `person` schema requires a date of birth, a 10-character phone and
  a valid email. Measured on the real RTP file, `emailaddre` and `birthdate` are
  empty on **100%** of rows and `phone` is present on **4.4%** — so reusing it
  would 400 roughly 96% of real requests. The service-layer `IntakePerson` has
  all three optional, which is what makes this work.

  ## Idempotency

  Keyed on `submissionToken = MAIL|<the mailer's normalized control-number key>`
  against the unique `{ agencyId, submissionToken }` index — the equivalent of
  legacy's `mail_log_token`, needing no new field.

  The key comes from the **mailer document**, not from the request. Keying on
  what the producer typed would make the two printed forms two different tokens,
  and one mailer would become two leads — see **Log Mailer Lead (Replay, Other
  Form)**.

  ## Response `200`

  ```json
  { "leadId": "68b0…", "alreadyExisted": false }
  ```

  `alreadyExisted` is `false` only when this call created the lead. It comes
  back `true` for three honest reasons, all reported the same way: a
  submission-token replay (this mailer logged before, in either form), the
  quote-control-number dedupe, and the address dedupe against a recent lead at
  the same street.

  > On a **fresh** demo seed this request creates the lead and
  > `alreadyExisted` is `false`; on a second collection run against the same
  > database it is `true` and the same `leadId` comes back. The assertions here
  > therefore pin the *shape* — the behavioural assertion lives in the replay
  > request, which is unconditional either way.

  **Errors**
  | code | when |
  |---|---|
  | `400` | missing or malformed `controlNumber` |
  | `403` | `mailers` or `leads` module disabled, or missing `mailers:read` / `leads:write` |
  | `404` | no mailer with that number in the caller's agency |
  | `422` | the mailer carries no usable recipient name, so no lead can be created — not `400` (the body was fine) and not `404` (the mailer exists) |
}
